<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/docs/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/docs/Code.abide'
import Section from '#src/docs/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <p>The handler carries the address, so the string is derived rather than typed. Rename the
    export and every place that reached for it follows, there being no path written down to
    miss.</p>

    <Section title="The four members an `Rpc` carries">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You read</th><th>What you get</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>rpc.url(args?)</code></td><td>the address, resolved against the mount at read time</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>rpc.method</code></td><td>the verb it was declared with</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>rpc.raw(args?, init?)</code></td><td>the <code>Response</code> itself, unparsed</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>rpc.description</code></td><td>the sentence the declaration gave it</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>None of the four is a call to the handler. They describe the address rather than reaching
        it, which is what makes them the surface for everything outside the app — a <code>&lt;form
        action&gt;</code>, an <code>&lt;img src&gt;</code>, a webhook registration, a
        <code>curl</code> line. Every signature is in the
        <a href={url('/reference/transports')}>Transports</a> reference.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`rpc.url` is derived, not baked">
        <p>The generated wrapper carries a <strong>mount-relative</strong> address. <code>url</code>
        resolves it at read time:</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>Against</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>a browser</td><td><code>&lt;meta name="abide-mount"&gt;</code> in the document</td></tr>
                <tr><td>a server</td><td><code>config().APP_URL</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>A mount is chosen after the build — <code>APP_URL</code> of
        <code>https://abide.com/v2</code> serves every page, endpoint and asset under
        <code>/v2</code> — so a build artifact cannot hold the absolute form. That is why the same
        bundle deploys at a sub-path without a rebuild.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`rpc.url` takes the args">
        <p>An address without its arguments is not something a browser can fetch, so the call takes
        them. They are written in the same canonical wire form the request itself uses, rather than
        a query string you assemble and the server parses back differently:</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="src/media/page.abide — excerpt" code={`<video src={playFile.url({ id: file.id })} controls></video>
<img src={getPoster.url({ path, width: 250 })} alt="">`} />

        <p>That is how a handler is reached from markup at all, and it is the only surface a handler
        serving bytes has. A parameter the route carries is written into the path, whatever the
        method; the rest go in the query on a <code>GET</code> or a <code>DELETE</code>, and in the
        body on a mutation.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/serve-the-app-under-a-sub-path')}>Sub-path mounting</a> ·
        <a href={url('/app/configure-the-app')}>Config</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`rpc.method` says which verb the address wants">
        <p>The method the handler was declared with, as a string. Reading it rather than writing
        <code>"post"</code> keeps a form correct when a handler is redeclared — and only
        <code>GET</code> and <code>POST</code> are form-reachable, so a <code>PUT</code> moved to a
        form is a bug the value makes visible.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/change-something-on-the-server')}>Mutations</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The address comes from the directory and the export name">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Export</th><th>Answers at</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>src/users/page.abide</code></td><td><code>getUser</code></td><td><code>/users/getUser</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/admin/page.abide</code></td><td><code>recent</code></td><td><code>/admin/recent</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/media/[id]/page.abide</code></td><td><code>save</code></td><td><code>/media/42/save</code>, with <code>id</code> in its args</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/media/[id]/comments.abide</code></td><td><code>addComment</code></td><td><code>/media/42/addComment</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>So the address is readable from the source tree, which is what a log line or a network
        panel is for. A parameter in the route reaches the handler's args from the path, so its
        <code>Args</code> declares it. The address is not a thing to write out: the route table is a
        build artifact, and two files that answer one method at one address are a build error naming
        both.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`rpc.raw` hands back the `Response`">
        <p>Where you want the <code>Response</code> rather than a decoded value — a header to read,
        a <code>Blob</code> to hand to a download, a body you will frame yourself:</p>

        <Code language="ts" caption="browser" code={`const response = await downloadLedger.raw(
    { year: 2026 },
    { headers: { accept: 'text/csv' } },
)
const disposition = response.headers.get('content-disposition')`} />

        <p>It takes a <code>RequestInit</code>, so it is also how a caller sends an
        <code>Accept</code> a streaming handler answers differently — jsonl or SSE at the same
        address.</p>

        <p>You give up everything the <code>Reactive</code> was doing: no coalescing, no
        <code>ttl</code>, no probes, no seeding. Reach for it when the response <strong>is</strong>
        the thing you want, and for the ordinary call otherwise.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/send-data-as-it-arrives')}>Streaming data</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/read-data-without-writing-an-api')}>Reading data</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`rpc.description` is the sentence every surface carries">
        <p>The string you passed as <code>description</code>, readable back off the handler. The
        same one rides onto the OpenAPI operation, the MCP tool and the CLI command — one place to
        write it, and no surface can carry a different sentence than another.</p>

        <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`export const getInvoice = GET(
    ({ id }: { id: string }) => database.invoice.find(id),
    { description: 'One invoice, by id.' },
)`} />

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/machines/describe-your-api-without-writing-a-spec')}>OpenAPI</a>
        · <a href={url('/machines/let-a-model-call-your-handlers')}>MCP</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A webhook takes `rpc.url` read on the server">
        <p>A provider that calls you wants an absolute URL, which is <code>rpc.url</code> read on
        the server:</p>

        <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`onStart(async (start) => {
    await payments.registerWebhook(paymentSettled.url())
    await start()
})`} />

        <p>The handler on the other end of that is an ordinary <code>POST</code>, and a caller that
        is not a browser sends no <code>Origin</code> — which the mutation gate allows on purpose,
        there being no ambient cookie to abuse. Authenticating that caller is a rung.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/decide-who-may-call-what')}>Authorization</a> ·
        <a href={url('/app/run-code-at-start-and-stop')}>Lifecycle</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/ship/call-a-handler-from-the-terminal')}>Call a handler</a> — the
            same handler from a shell</li>
            <li><a href={url('/pages/serve-the-app-under-a-sub-path')}>Sub-path mounting</a> — why
            the address is relative</li>
            <li><a href={url('/machines/keep-a-handler-off-a-surface')}>Withholding a surface</a> —
            an address that answers but is not listed</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
